// traditional: string_last_of_or_end
//
// Not the string_last_of_or_end of the riscv64 word loop, which is a different function at one
// input: string_last_of_or_end(source, 0) is the terminator there and null here.
// Performs a single cut forward in a string by inserting a null terminator where the FIRST cut symbol is found.
// Returns the address AFTER the cut, effectively splitting the string into two parts.
// searching starts from the beginning of the string,
// linearly steps forward until a cut symbol is found or a string end is reached.
//
// # example:
//      string_address input = "Hello World";
//      string_address second_part = string_cut(input, ' ');
//      // input = "Hello"
//      // second_part = "World"
// returns the the start address of the first occurrence input, null if not found
/*
        The value of a name in an environment.

        The list is null terminated and each entry reads NAME=value, which is
        what execve is handed and what a shell keeps. The answer points into
        the entry, one byte past the equals, so nothing is copied.

        A name that is not there answers null, and so does a name whose entry
        has no equals in it -- the same two answers the shell's own loop gave.
*/
